by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-20T10:58:20Z

Thanks for the rework, and for dropping the Form side yourself, that was the right call.

I pushed the last round on top of your branch, rebased on 8.2. What changed:

**Exponent notation was silently comparing the wrong way.** `(string) 0.0000001` is `"1.0E-7"`, which `BcMath\Number` refuses, so the conversion fell back to the native comparison, and the native comparison truncates the float to an integer:

```php
$validator->validate(new \BcMath\Number('0.00000005'), new GreaterThanOrEqual(0.0000001));
// no violation, and PHP reports "Implicit conversion from float 1.0E-7 to int loses precision"
```

Every float below roughly `1e-5` or above `1e15` is written in exponent notation by the string cast, at the default `precision`, and numeric strings such as `'1.0E-7'` hit the same path. The conversion now rewrites those to plain decimal first, so they convert like any other limit. Trailing zeros of the mantissa are trimmed, otherwise the message would read `0.00000010`.

**The two conversions are now one helper.** `RangeValidator::toNumber()` and the inline block in `AbstractComparisonValidator` did the same thing, so both use an `@internal` trait. Five tests were added for the small float, large float and exponent string cases, in both validators.

**Smaller things:** the changelog entry is one line, and two test comments described the native behavior as a coercion. It is a truncation to integer, which is why `Number('10.2') > 10.5` is true, so they now say that.

The description is rewritten as documentation, including what stays out: `DivisibleBy`, the identity constraints and the Form side.

